Deep Research Dossier: Commenting on Serge Dauchy’s Analysis of Montesquieu’s De l’esprit des lois and the Paradox of Gender
Commenting effectively on Professor Serge Dauchy’s study, *"Montesquieu, De l’esprit des lois (1748)"*1, requires positioning your intervention along two complementary axes: first, directly answering the comparative-law and Brazilian historiographical questions that Dauchy raises in his text ("the fresh ideas"); and second, integrating a rigorous textual and philosophical analysis of Montesquieu’s treatment of women ("the paradox of gender"), a theme absent from Dauchy’s main biographical-institutional narrative but central to the analytical appendix accompanying the Portuguese translation1. Because Dauchy writes as a European legal historian tracing the genesis, global circulation, and comparative-law legacy of De l’esprit des lois, he explicitly concludes his essay by deferring to Brazilian legal scholars on a specific historical question: how Clóvis Beviláqua and the drafters of the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code navigated Montesquieu’s problem of legal transplants and institutional hybridity2.
Addressing Dauchy’s closing invitation provides the strongest structural hook for your commentary2. By demonstrating how Clóvis Beviláqua—Professor of Comparative Legislation at the Faculty of Law of Recife (Escola do Recife)—synthesized Montesquieu’s climatic and cultural relativism (esprit général) with Rudolf von Jhering’s legal teleology and comparative taxonomy, you can resolve the theoretical standoff Dauchy outlines between Alan Watson’s decontextualized "legal transplants" and Pierre Legrand’s "impossibility thesis"2. Furthermore, you can enrich Dauchy’s briefer references to Montesquieu’s clandestine circulation in colonial Brazil (1789 and 1798), the 1823–1824 constitutional debates, Catherine the Great’s 1767 Nakaz, and Montesquieu’s criminal-law "system of liberty" with primary archival evidence2.
Crucially, these two halves of your research—Dauchy’s comparative-law framework and Montesquieu’s gender theory—converge directly within the drafting history of the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code1. In De l’esprit des lois, Montesquieu treats the condition of women as a political "thermometer" of regimes while simultaneously invoking physiological determinism and "natural weakness" (faiblesse) to justify women’s domestic guardianship under private law, even as he praises their moderation as heads of state (Book VII, Ch. 17; Book XVI, Ch. 2)1. When Clóvis Beviláqua drafted his 1899 Projeto Primitivo for the Brazilian Civil Code, he attempted to break with this patriarchal tutelage by excluding married women from the list of legally incapable persons (Article 6)11. However, conservative parliamentarians led by Domingos de Andrade Figueira reinstated the relative civil incapacity of married women to preserve traditional marital hierarchy—a legal subordination that endured in Brazil until the Estatuto da Mulher Casada (Law No. 4.121 of 1962)3. Connecting Dauchy’s inquiry into the 1916 Code with Montesquieu’s gender paradox transforms two separate topics into a unified, original scholarly commentary.
Part I: Fresh Ideas and Original Avenues for Commenting on Serge Dauchy’s Text
In his paper, Professor Dauchy moves from the intellectual biography of Charles-Louis de Secondat, baron de La Brède et de Montesquieu (1689–1755), to the transatlantic reception of De l’esprit des lois (1748) in North America and Brazil, before culminating in a theoretical examination of legal transplants (transplantations juridiques)2. To offer a high-impact scholarly commentary rather than a summary, your analysis can advance four original arguments that directly expand, nuance, or answer the propositions in Dauchy’s text.

1. Answering Dauchy’s Closing Question: Clóvis Beviláqua, the Escola do Recife, and the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code
At the conclusion of his essay, Serge Dauchy frames Montesquieu as the progenitor of the modern debate over legal transplants (transplantations juridiques)2. Quoting Book I, Chapter 3 of De l’esprit des lois—where Montesquieu declares that political and civil laws must be so specific to the people for whom they are made that "it is a very great chance if those of one nation can suit another" ("c’est un très-grand hasard si celles d’une nation peuvent convenir à une autre")—Dauchy contrasts two modern poles of comparative law2. On one side stands Alan Watson’s autonomous transfer thesis in Legal Transplants (1974): inspired by Christiaan Barnard’s late-1960s heart transplants, Watson argued that there is no necessary organic link between laws and the society in which they operate, meaning legal rules are easily borrowed across borders and evolve within the host system without implying "rejection"2. On the opposite side stands Pierre Legrand’s cultural contextualism and "impossibility thesis": following Montesquieu’s spatial sensibility and Max Weber’s insistence on defining the historical individuality of each development, Legrand contends that a legal rule cannot exist apart from its culturally embedded meaning, rendering true legal transplants "impossible"2.
Dauchy observes that contemporary comparatists largely transcend this binary by recognizing legal hybridity and citing Gunther Teubner’s thesis that institutional transfers occur not between isolated national societies, but via "direct contact between legal orders within a global legal discourse"2. In his seminal 1998 article "Legal Irritants", Teubner argues that transplanted rules neither integrate seamlessly (as Watson claims) nor simply fail (as Legrand claims), but act as "irritants" that trigger new, unexpected evolutionary dynamics within the host legal culture19. Dauchy closes his text by pointing to the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code, drafted by Clóvis Beviláqua (Professor of Civil and Comparative Law at Recife), and explicitly asks his Brazilian colleagues whether the question of legal transplants raised by Montesquieu was debated during the Code's long drafting and adoption process2.
Your commentary can answer Dauchy’s question affirmatively by demonstrating three specific mechanisms from Brazilian legal historiography. First, prior to being invited in 1899 to draft the Civil Code, Beviláqua taught Comparative Legislation at the Faculty of Law of Recife, publishing his pioneering Resumo das Lições de Legislação Comparada sobre o Direito Privado (1st ed. 1893; 2nd ed. 1897)4. Steeped in the intellectual movement of the Escola do Recife—which viewed law as an evolving cultural product anchored in its historical and social environment4—Beviláqua explicitly engaged with Montesquieu’s De l’esprit des lois (including Book I, Ch. 3) and examined how physical determinants (such as climate, relief, and hydrography) interact with contacts between nations to shape legal institutions5.
Second, in his comparative treatise, Beviláqua classified legal systems into large groups or "families" (grandes grupos, as famílias), tracing their historical roots and social conditioning4. As legal historian Mario Losano highlights, Beviláqua’s classification was original and non-Eurocentric ("Clóvis Beviláqua não é eurocêntrico")27: he recognized Latin American legal systems as an autonomous, constitutively hybrid legal family that combined European Roman-canon heritage with modern constitutional and codification movements adapted to American social realities3.
Third, when Beviláqua drafted his Civil Code project in six months in 1899 (promulgated as Law No. 3.071 on January 1, 1916, under President Venceslau Brás)3, the parliamentary and doctrinal debates directly confronted the tension between foreign legal borrowing and Brazilian social reality3. Critics scrutinized the project’s foreign influences—debating whether its division into a Parte Geral and Parte Especial reflected German BGB pandectism (though in Brazil this structure had already developed domestically from Augusto Teixeira de Freitas, and Beviláqua refused to unify civil and commercial law into a single code) or whether its syntax was overly Frenchified (sparking the famous grammatical polemic with Rui Barbosa and Ernesto Carneiro Ribeiro)3. In Em Defesa do Projeto de Código Civil Brasileiro (1906), Beviláqua defended his methodology by arguing that codes operate as true "philosophical systems" (sistemas filosóficos) synthesizing a worldview23. Influenced by Rudolf von Jhering’s teleological view of the "social function" of legal institutes3, Beviláqua maintained that comparative legislation had to be filtered through Brazilian "benignidade jurídica" (legal benignity), which softened the harshness of archaic Portuguese Ordenações while giving the law enough elasticity not to obstruct social evolution3. Thus, Beviláqua’s 1916 Code exemplifies Teubner’s and Dauchy’s thesis: it was forged through direct contact with global legal discourse, consciously hybridizing comparative European models with Luso-Brazilian social and family traditions2.
