Inconfidência Mineira (1789)
Notes that Montesquieu's writings nourished discussions during the 1789 conspiracy in Minas Gerais, introduced by young aristocrats studying at Coimbra or in France2.
Primary evidence comes from the Autos de Sequestro (confiscation inventories) in the Autos da Devassa da Inconfidência Mineira7. The largest private library seized belonged to Canon Luís Vieira da Silva in Mariana: original manuscript sequestration records document 267 books (titles) in 569 volumes, featuring Montesquieu alongside Condillac, Voltaire, Diderot, Rousseau, d'Alembert, Mably, Raynal, Turgot, Burlamaqui, and Vatel7. Tiradentes and fellow conspirators frequented Vieira da Silva's library, where Enlightenment political works fueled opposition to colonial taxation and arbitrary governance41. Additionally, the satirical Cartas Chilenas circulated in Vila Rica prior to 1789, drawing structural inspiration from Montesquieu42.
Conjuração Baiana / Revolt of the Tailors (1798)
Cites the confiscation inventory of surgeon and philosopher Cipriano Barata in Salvador da Bahia (1798), listing De l'esprit des lois, the Encyclopédie, and Voltaire2.
Historian Kátia Queirós Mattoso (Presença francesa no movimento democrático baiano de 1798) analyzed the seized private libraries of both Cipriano Barata (who owned 74 books) and Lieutenant Hermógenes Francisco de Aguilar Pantoja6. The inventories confirm the presence of both Montesquieu’s De l’esprit des lois (Paris: Pierre Didot l'aîné, et Firmin Didot) and his Lettres persanes (Amsterdam edition) alongside Voltaire, Rousseau, Raynal, Locke, Volney, Franklin, and Paine6. Crucially, in 1798 Salvador, Enlightenment ideas crossed class and racial boundaries, as French texts owned by physicians and officers informed a broader movement involving free mulatto artisans, tailors, and enslaved workers via seditious handwritten pasquins50.
1823 Constituent Assembly & 1824 Imperial Constitution
Notes that De l'esprit des lois was abundantly cited in the 1823 Constituent Assembly and that the 1824 Constitution adopted Benjamin Constant’s four-power model as an evolution or "correction" of Montesquieu's tripartition2.
In the 1823 Assembleia Constituinte, deputies such as Antônio Carlos Ribeiro de Andrada, José Joaquim Carneiro de Campos (future Marquis of Caravelas), and the reactionary José da Silva Lisboa (Viscount of Cairu) debated how to balance monarchical authority and constitutional liberty52. After Emperor Dom Pedro I dissolved the Assembly on November 12, 1823, Carneiro de Campos drafted the Constitution of March 1824, incorporating Benjamin Constant’s Principes de politique (1814)10. However, as Brazilian political historians demonstrate, the 1824 Charter modified Constant’s liberal theory: whereas Constant conceived the Pouvoir Moderador as strictly distinct from the Executive, Articles 98 and 101 (§ VI) of the 1824 Brazilian Constitution vested both the Moderating Power and the headship of the Executive Power in the Emperor, giving the monarch active interventionist authority (including dissolving the Chamber of Deputies)10.

3. Beyond "Pure Constitutionalism": The Penal "System of Liberty" (Book XII) and Catherine II’s Nakaz
Two further insights in Dauchy’s paper invite deeper textual and historical commentary. First, Dauchy warns that locking Montesquieu into a purely "constitutionalist" vision—as North American reception tended to do by focusing on the constitutional engineering of separation of powers—is reductive2. For Montesquieu, constitutional separation of powers (séparation et équilibre des pouvoirs) is insufficient unless seconded by civil and criminal laws and a judicial framework that guarantee the individual citizen a concrete "system of liberty" (système de liberté)2. You can ground this observation textually by contrasting Book XI ("Of the Laws Which Establish Political Liberty with Regard to the Constitution") with Book XII ("Of the Laws That Form Political Liberty, in Relation to the Citizen")62. In Book XII, Chapter 2, Montesquieu formulates his famous penal maxim:
"This security is never more dangerously attacked than in public or private accusations. It is therefore on the goodness of criminal laws that the liberty of the citizen principally depends." ("C’est donc de la bonté des lois criminelles que dépend principalement la liberté du citoyen")8.
This connects directly back to Dauchy’s opening observation on the Lettres persanes (1721), where Montesquieu used his Persian travelers to attack the cruelty of France’s Ordonnance criminelle of 1670 and the absurdity of prosecuting suicides2. By linking Book VI (proportionality of punishments) and Book XII (procedural guarantees against arbitrary accusations of lèse-majesté and crimes against nature) to Cesare Beccaria and the Milanese Enlightenment8, your commentary shows why Montesquieu viewed criminal procedure—not just constitutional parchments—as the primary bulwark of everyday civic freedom2.
Second, Dauchy highlights the reception of De l’esprit des lois among "enlightened monarchs," noting that half of the 655 articles of Empress Catherine II of Russia’s 1767 Nakaz (Instruction to the Legislative Commission) were copied word-for-word from Montesquieu, whom she called her "breviary" in a June 1765 letter to d’Alembert explaining that she had "pillaged President Montesquieu without naming him"2. Your commentary can expose the geopolitical and ideological subversion behind Catherine’s selective borrowing2. Whereas Montesquieu analyzed Muscovy through the lens of vast climate-bound empires and criticized tyrannical methods of altering mœurs such as cuts to beards (Book XIX, Ch. 14; Book XI)68, Catherine II opened Article 6 of the Nakaz with the peremptory declaration: "Russia is a European power" ("La Russie est une puissance européenne" / "Россия есть Европейская держава")67. While copying roughly half of her 655 clauses from Montesquieu (alongside clauses from Beccaria)2, she carefully altered the text to fit her vision of Russia as an absolutist bureaucratic monarchy, tactfully ignoring Montesquieu’s division of powers and three estates in order to strengthen autocracy9.
Part II: Deep Research on Montesquieu’s Views on Women and the Paradox of Gender
While Professor Dauchy’s main text focuses on constitutionalism, climate, and comparative legal transfers, the analytical appendix accompanying the Portuguese translation ("Apêndice Analítico: O Paradoxo de Gênero em Montesquieu") addresses one of the richest tensions in Enlightenment thought: Montesquieu’s structural ambivalence toward women1. Across Lettres persanes (1721) and De l’esprit des lois (1748—especially Books VII, XVI, and XIX), Montesquieu treats the status of women as a central sociological diagnostic tool ("thermometer") of political regimes, yet simultaneously deploys climatic and biological determinism to justify women’s legal guardianship in the domestic sphere1.

1. Women as a Diagnostic "Thermometer" of Political Regimes (Books VII & XIX)
In Book VII of De l’esprit des lois ("Consequences of the Different Principles of the Three Governments with Respect to Sumptuary Laws, Luxury, and the Condition of Women"), Montesquieu establishes that the legal and social status of women is not an accidental cultural trait, but a direct structural consequence of each regime’s animating principle (principe)1. Under Despotism, whose principle is fear (crainte), women are reduced to items of extreme luxury and subjected to total confinement within the seraglio or harem1. Domestic female slavery mirrors and stabilizes general political servitude, preventing female intrigues from destabilizing arbitrary power1.
Under Monarchy, which operates on honor (honneur), social distinction, and luxury, women enjoy broad social freedom at court1. Although Montesquieu portrays courtly women as agents of frivolity, fashion, and gallantry, he considers this "vice" politically and economically useful1. As he remarks in Book XIX, Chapter 8, sociable communication with women "spoils morals and forms taste" ("gâte les mœurs et forme le goût")68: female vanity and changing fashions foster polite manners (mœurs and politesse), stimulate commerce, and drive the circulation of wealth1.
Conversely, in a Republic, which requires civic virtue (vertu—self-denial, austerity, and frugal equality), female luxury and behavioral license are viewed as direct vectors of political corruption1. In Book VII, Chapter 9, Montesquieu formulates his famous republican synthesis:
"In republics, women are free by the laws and captured [constrained] by morals; luxury is banished from them, and with it corruption and vices." ("Dans les républiques, les femmes sont libres par les lois, et captivées par les mœurs ; le luxe en est banni, et avec lui la corruption et les vices")63.
Consequently, while women in republics are formally free in the public sphere, Montesquieu argues that their dress, chastity, and conduct must be strictly monitored by censors, domestic tribunals, and severe sumptuary laws (Book VII, Ch. 9–14)1.
2. The "Empire of Climate," Physiological Determinism, and Servitude Domestique (Book XVI)
When Montesquieu turns from political principles to physical causes in Part III of De l’esprit des lois (Books XIV–XVII), he introduces a stark physiological determinism1. In Book XVI, Chapter 1, he distinguishes chattel slavery (esclavage civil) from the subordination of women in the household and polygamy, which he terms "domestic servitude" (servitude domestique)13.
In Book XVI, Chapter 2 ("That in the Countries of the South There Is a Natural Inequality between the Two Sexes"), Montesquieu constructs a biological-chronological argument to explain why polygamy and female subordination prevail in hot climates while monogamy and greater marital symmetry prevail in temperate and cold zones1:
"Women, in hot climates, are marriageable at eight, nine, and ten years of age; thus, childhood and marriage almost always go together. They are old at twenty: therefore, reason in women is never found together with beauty. When beauty demands the empire, the want of reason forbids the claim; when reason is obtained, beauty is no more. These women ought then to be in a state of dependence; for reason cannot procure in old age that empire which even youth and beauty could not give." ("Les femmes sont nubiles, dans les climats chauds, à huit, neuf et dix ans : ainsi l'enfance et le mariage y vont presque toujours ensemble. Elles sont vieilles à vingt : la raison ne se trouve donc jamais chez elles avec la beauté...")1.
By contrast, Montesquieu argues that in temperate climates (such as Europe), women’s physical charms are preserved longer and they marry at a more mature age when they possess greater knowledge and reason; consequently, the aging of husband and wife is synchronized, grounding a natural equality that supports Christian monogamy13. In cold climates, where men are driven to intemperance by the heavy consumption of strong liquor, women’s natural defensive restraint (retenue naturelle) even gives them an advantage in reason over men13.
Furthermore, in Book XVI, Chapters 8 to 11, Montesquieu argues that extreme heat inflames the passions and imagination to such a degree that internal moral norms falter1. Referring to accounts of India and hot climates, he writes in Book XVI, Chapter 8:
"There are such climates where the physical part has such strength that morality can do almost nothing." ("Il y a de tels climats où le physique a une telle force que la morale n'y peut presque rien")77.
From this premise, he deduces (Book XVI, Ch. 8–11) that in hot Oriental climates, female morality can only be contained through physical barriers—walls and bolts—because women have so many duties proper to the family that "one cannot separate them enough from everything that could give them other ideas" ("Les femmes ont naturellement à remplir tant de devoirs qui leur sont propres, qu'on ne peut assez les séparer de tout ce qui pourrait leur donner d'autres idées", Book XVI, Ch. 10)1. In Northern and temperate Europe, conversely, the physiological "coolness" of humors acts as a natural restraint, allowing women to circulate freely in society1.
3. The Paradox of Sovereignty: Unfit in the Home, Excellent on the Throne (Book VII, Ch. 17)
The most striking tension in Montesquieu’s gender theory appears at the close of Book VII, in Chapter 17, titled De l’administration des femmes ("Of Female Administration")1. Here Montesquieu formulates a paradoxical rule that inverts traditional patriarchal analogies between the household and the state1:
"It is contrary to reason and contrary to nature for women to be mistresses in the house, as was established among the Egyptians; but it is not at all so for them to govern an empire. In the first case, the state of weakness in which they find themselves does not allow them preeminence; in the second, their very weakness gives them more gentleness and moderation, which can make for a good government, rather than the hard and ferocious virtues." ("Il est contre la raison et contre la nature que les femmes soient maîtresses dans la maison [...] ; mais il ne l'est pas qu'elles gouvernent un empire. Dans le premier cas, l'état de faiblesse où elles sont ne leur permet pas la prééminence ; dans le second, leur faiblesse même leur donne plus de douceur et de modération, ce qui peut faire un bon gouvernement, plutôt que les vertus dures et féroces")1.
To substantiate this claim empirically, Montesquieu cites the examples of India (les Indes), Muscovy (la Moscovie / Russia), and England (l'Angleterre), concluding that female sovereigns *"succeed equally well in both moderate government and despotic government"*1. Against divine-right theorists (such as Bossuet in France) who modeled royal sovereignty on paternal household rule, Montesquieu decouples private domestic law from public state sovereignty1. While women’s perceived "natural weakness" (faiblesse) deprives them of domestic preeminence and justifies their civil subordination to their husbands under private law, that exact same quality on the throne fosters douceur (gentleness), modération (Montesquieu’s supreme political virtue), and a willingness to listen to advisors rather than the "hard and fierce" virtues of men1.
4. Proto-Feminist Subversion and Natural Law in Lettres persanes (1721)
Any comprehensive commentary on Montesquieu’s views on women must contrast the institutional and climatic determinism of De l’esprit des lois (1748) with the radical critiques of patriarchal tyranny voiced twenty-seven years earlier in his anonymous epistolary novel, Lettres persanes (1721)1. Through his fictional correspondents, the younger Montesquieu articulated arguments for sexual equality that stand in sharp tension with his later treatise1.
In Letter 38, the Persian traveler Rica writes to Ibben from Paris, questioning whether natural law genuinely subjects women to men and quoting a philosopher who argues that male dominion is a tyranny based purely on brute physical force and unequal education1:
"Why then should we have a privilege? Is it because we are the strongest? But that is a veritable injustice. We employ all sorts of means to beat down their courage; forces would be equal if education were equal too." ("Pourquoi aurions-nous donc un privilège ? Est-ce parce que nous sommes les plus forts ? Mais c'est une véritable injustice. Nous employons toutes sortes de moyens pour leur abattre le courage ; les forces seraient égales si l'éducation l'était aussi")1.
Even more dramatically, Letter 161—placed strategically as the final letter (excipit) of the entire novel—gives the last word to Roxane, Usbek’s favorite wife in the seraglio of Isfahan1. Caught with her lover (who is killed by the eunuchs), Roxane poisons herself and pens a devastating suicide note to Usbek asserting her inner moral autonomy under natural law1:
"Yes, I have deceived you; I have seduced your eunuchs; I have made sport of your jealousy; and I have known how to turn your frightful seraglio into a place of delights and pleasures... How could you think that I was credulous enough to imagine that I was in the world only to adore your caprices? that while you permitted yourself everything, you had the right to afflict all my desires? No: I was able to live in servitude, but I was always free: I reformed your laws according to those of nature, and my spirit always remained in independence." ("Oui, je t'ai trompé ; j'ai séduit tes eunuques ; je me suis jouée de ta jalousie ; et j'ai su de ton affreux sérail faire un lieu de délices et de plaisirs... Non : j'ai pu vivre dans la servitude ; mais j'ai toujours été libre : j'ai réformé tes lois sur celles de la nature ; et mon esprit s'est toujours tenu dans l'indépendance")1.
5. Historiographical Debates and Dual Afterlives: Revolutionary France (1793) and the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code
Modern scholarship on Montesquieu and gender has extensively examined the tension between the emancipatory voices of 1721 and the sociological determinism of 1748. Foundational studies include Jeannette Geffriaud Rosso’s Montesquieu et la féminité (1977)90 and Pauline Kra’s "Montesquieu and Women" (1984), which situates his analysis of the harem and female status within his broader critique of despotism82. Diana Schaub, in Erotic Liberalism: Women and Revolution in Montesquieu’s Persian Letters (1995) and "Montesquieu on the Liberty of Women" (in The Cambridge Companion to Montesquieu), argues that Lettres persanes critiques domestic, political, and religious despotism simultaneously by recognizing human beings as erotic creatures91. In French political philosophy, Catherine Larrère has traced how Montesquieu’s distinction between the polite doux commerce of monarchy and the austere republican model of female citizenship influenced Jean-Jacques Rousseau74, while Céline Spector (Montesquieu: Pouvoirs, richesses et sociétés; Servitude et Empire: Montesquieu, des Lettres persanes à L’Esprit des lois, 2024) demonstrates how Montesquieu analyzes the government of the family alongside the legal status of women as persons, property holders, or objects of domestic empire84.
Finally, your commentary can trace two concrete historical and legal afterlives of Montesquieu’s gender paradox—one in Revolutionary France and one in Brazil that ties directly back to Serge Dauchy’s concluding question:
During the French Revolution, Jacobin revolutionaries operationalized Montesquieu’s distinction between the "luxurious courtly woman of the Monarchy" and the "chaste, monitored woman of the Republic" (Book VII, Ch. 8–9)1. On October 30, 1793 (9 Brumaire Year II), Deputy Jean-Pierre-André Amar, presenting a decree on behalf of the Committee of General Security to outlaw all women’s political clubs, echoed Montesquieu’s arguments on natural domestic duties and female modesty (retenue)1:
"The private functions to which women are destined by nature itself belong to the general order of what exists between man and woman. Each sex is called to a kind of occupation that is proper to it; its action is circumscribed within this circle which it cannot cross... Do you want, in the French Republic, to see [women] come to the bar, to the speaker’s tribune, to political assemblies, abandoning both the modesty [retenue] that is the source of all the virtues of this sex and the care of their family?"97.
Thus, in 1793, the French Republic banned women’s political clubs and demanded austere domesticity from real women, while simultaneously elevating an abstract, desexualized, or maternal female figure (Marianne, Justice, the Republic) as the symbolic center of state sovereignty1.
You can bring your commentary to a powerful synthesis by showing how this same tension between progressive equality and domestic tutelage played out in the drafting of the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code highlighted by Professor Dauchy2. Personally committed to women’s intellectual equality—he was married to the writer Amélia de Freitas Beviláqua and famously stopped attending the Brazilian Academy of Letters when it refused her candidacy24—Clóvis Beviláqua drafted his 1899 Projeto Primitivo without including married women among the legally incapable in Article 6 ("a incapacidade da mulher casada não fora articulada no projeto primitivo")11. However, during the parliamentary review process, conservative jurists led by Domingos de Andrade Figueira (alongside Lacerda de Almeida and Penaforte Mendes) intervened to preserve traditional Catholic family hierarchy and marital headship3. Overriding Beviláqua’s original design, Congress inserted married women into Article 6, II of the 1916 Civil Code as relatively incapable—grouped alongside indigenous persons (índios), prodigals (pródigos), and minors—while granting sole command of the family to the husband3. Left to rationalize a statutory restriction he had not included in his original draft, Beviláqua argued in his doctrinal commentaries (Teoria Geral do Direito Civil) that the married woman’s legal restriction did not stem from her sex or any natural weakness, but solely from the administrative unity of the household—a patriarchal guardianship that persisted in Brazilian private law until the Statute of the Married Woman (Law No. 4.121 of August 27, 1962) abolished the relative incapacity of married women3.
By weaving this legislative history into your response to Professor Dauchy, you demonstrate how the 1916 Brazilian Civil Code serves not only as a prime laboratory for Montesquieu’s theory of legal transplants and cultural hybridity, but also as the exact institutional arena where Montesquieu’s paradox of gender was contested in modern Brazilian law1.
